# Exact per-finding fingerprints for reviewed historical false positives, read
# by gitleaks by default from this file at the repo root (`-i`/
# `--gitleaks-ignore-path` defaults to "."; see `gitleaks --help`).
#
# This replaces the prior in-config regex allowlist ("Git commit/tree ids
# referenced in blind-catalog-convergence review narrative", duplicated under
# the `generic-api-key` and `sourcegraph-access-token` rules in
# .gitleaks.toml) after four review rounds (Codex cross-family review of PR
# #83; the Opus evidence review of this branch, not codex-review-72; a
# further blind Codex cross-family review of PR #83; a blind Opus/Codex
# evidence review of that round-3 fix) each found the prose-narrative regex
# allowlist leaky in a new way:
#   - round 2: an unrelated key:value pair sharing the physical line was
#     wrongly exempted.
#   - round 3: a secret-shaped substring inside the SAME JSON string as the
#     commit reference was still exempted.
#   - round 4: the widened marker-hex length window re-exempted a marker word
#     used as ordinary prose (e.g. "source"/"pinned") immediately followed by
#     an unrelated 41-64-char hex secret.
#   - a final review (codex-review-83b) found the marker's
#     `[\sa-zA-Z0-9_./:,\-]{0,25}` separator can itself absorb leading hex
#     characters (so `"detail": "source <64hex>"` still passes as a false
#     "short abbreviated hash" match), and that a non-hex-shaped secret
#     sharing the same JSON string as a real commit/pin/tree/source reference
#     remains exempted in full (documented as an open residual, not closed,
#     in the round-3/round-4 regexes this file replaces).
# RE2 (gitleaks' regex engine) has no lookaround or backreferences, so a
# regex-based fix cannot close these gaps: an allowlist rule that exempts
# "a commit id preceded by a marker word, anywhere in the string" cannot also
# assert "and nothing else non-hex/non-decimal shares that string" without a
# negative lookahead RE2 does not support.
#
# Fingerprints below are exact `commit:path:rule:line` entries (gitleaks' own
# git-mode fingerprint format), generated once, by hand, against each of this
# repository's own reviewed narrative lines. A fingerprint only matches its
# exact commit, so any secret added in a LATER commit -- even on the exact
# same line number in the same file -- has a different fingerprint and is
# still detected; this is strictly narrower than the regex allowlist it
# replaces, which matched by shape/context on any commit that touched a
# matching line.
#
# Generated 2026-09-22 with the guarded, memory-capped `gitleaks` launcher on
# PATH (never the underlying binary, never with raised limits) using a scratch
# copy of .gitleaks.toml with both narrative-allowlist `[[rules.allowlists]]`
# blocks removed, confirming every remaining finding fell inside the two
# narrative files before any fingerprint was recorded here:
#   gitleaks git . --config <scratch-config-without-narrative-allowlists> \
#     --max-target-megabytes 2 --redact --no-banner --exit-code 0 \
#     --log-opts=HEAD --report-format json --report-path git.json
#   gitleaks dir . --config <scratch-config-without-narrative-allowlists> \
#     --max-target-megabytes 2 --redact --no-banner --exit-code 0 \
#     --report-format json --report-path dir.json
# (git.json: 15 findings / 14 unique fingerprints, all in the two narrative
# files below; dir.json: 0 findings.)
#
# Rule: add a fingerprint here ONLY for a reviewed false positive (a narrative
# reference to a commit/tree id, never an actual live credential). Never add a
# fingerprint for a real secret. A finding for a real secret must be rotated
# and removed from history, not ignored.
#
# Another branch may add its own .gitleaksignore entries for an unrelated
# finding; this file is kept easy to merge -- one fingerprint per line,
# grouped under a comment naming the source file.

